          "dossier": {
            "en": "The FN FAL 50.00 is the standard fixed-stock variant of the Belgian FN FAL, designed by Dieudonné Saive at Fabrique Nationale (FN Herstal) and introduced in 1953. Chambered in 7.62×51mm NATO, it features a 533 mm (21-inch) barrel, a 20-round detachable magazine, and a short-stroke gas-piston operating system with a tilting breechblock. It was developed as a full-power infantry battle rifle and became one of the most widely adopted military rifles of the Cold War.FN FAL with wooden handguard and stock",
            "tc": "The FN FAL 50.00 is the standard fixed-stock variant of the Belgian FN FAL, designed by Dieudonné Saive at Fabrique Nationale (FN Herstal) and introduced in 1953. Chambered in 7.62×51mm NATO, it features a 533 mm (21-inch) barrel, a 20-round detachable magazine, and a short-stroke gas-piston operating system with a tilting breechblock. It was developed as a full-power infantry battle rifle and became one of the most widely adopted military rifles of the Cold War.FN FAL with wooden handguard and stock",
            "sc": "The FN FAL 50.00 is the standard fixed-stock variant of the Belgian FN FAL, designed by Dieudonné Saive at Fabrique Nationale (FN Herstal) and introduced in 1953. Chambered in 7.62×51mm NATO, it features a 533 mm (21-inch) barrel, a 20-round detachable magazine, and a short-stroke gas-piston operating system with a tilting breechblock. It was developed as a full-power infantry battle rifle and became one of the most widely adopted military rifles of the Cold War.FN FAL with wooden handguard and stock"
          }
